<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernhard Riemann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1826">1826</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Georg Friedrich Bernhard Riemann</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernhard Riemann was a German mathematician, born in 1826 at Breselenz in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, who made contributions to analysis, number theory and differential geometry. The son of a poor Lutheran pastor, he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a> in 1846 to study theology but turned to mathematics under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-friedrich-gauss">Carl Friedrich Gauss</a>. He gave the first rigorous formulation of the integral, introduced Riemann surfaces, and in an 1859 paper on the prime-counting function stated the Riemann hypothesis; his lectures of 1854 founded Riemannian geometry, later the mathematics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">general relativity</a>. He died of tuberculosis at Selasca in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1866.</p></div>
